# Line endings, settled once.
#
# Git already stores text as LF. Without this file, what lands in the working
# tree is left to core.autocrlf — true on a Windows clone, so every checkout
# rewrites LF to CRLF, while the build scripts, Capacitor and Gradle keep
# writing LF back. Neither side is wrong and neither gives way, so git warns
# about files nobody edited, on every add, on every commit.
#
# Pinning the working tree to LF ends it: what the tools write is what git sees,
# on every platform. Nothing in this repository is edited by a tool that cannot
# read LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Except Windows batch files. cmd.exe can mis-parse a .bat whose lines end in
# LF alone — labels and goto are the usual casualties — and android/gradlew.bat
# ships with the Gradle wrapper.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary, so git never inspects them for line endings, never converts them, and
# never tries to diff them as text. The flags and map renders are full of bytes
# that look like CR to a naive scan.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff2     binary
*.jar       binary
*.aab       binary
*.apk       binary
*.jks       binary
*.keystore  binary
